No requirement in this system has ever been parseable, and here is the whole reason.

WHERE IT SHOWED. MoneyMoney fails code signing with errSecCSResourcesInvalid, the sealed resource
directory is invalid, and that points at the application plist. It is not the plist. The seal parses
perfectly: 499 files and 13 rules. The throw is in validateNestedCode, where the designated
requirement sealing each piece of nested code is parsed:

    CIDER_CSSTEP nested code requirement REFUSED rc=-50 for: anchor apple generic and identifier
    UpdateHelper and (certificate leaf[field.1.2.840.113635.100.6.1.9] ... subject.OU] = "9BE2AB75LL")

That is an ordinary Apple designated requirement. The same parser had already refused a FIXED,
Apple-authored string inside isAppleDeveloperCert with the same -50, so this is ONE defect appearing
twice and not two coincidences.

THE REQUIREMENT LANGUAGE IS PARSED BY A LOADABLE BUNDLE, csparser.bundle, inside Security.framework.
Four separate things were wrong, each hiding the next:

  1. Security.framework HAS NO Info.plist, so CFBundleGetBundleWithIdentifier("com.apple.security")
     returned NULL and the plugin path could never even be computed.
  2. There is no PlugIns symlink at the framework root, so the computed path
     Security.framework/PlugIns/csparser.bundle did not resolve.
  3. csparser.bundle was never INSTALLED into the framework at all, though it is built.
  4. And with the first three fixed by hand, the plugin loads and the entry point still does not
     resolve, because THE BUNDLE IS 4112 BYTES OF NOTHING: csparser.cpp is twenty-three lines whose
     entire body is asm(".reference _findAntlrPlugin"). It is a stub that REFERENCES the symbol; the
     buck target links the stub and none of the ANTLR sources that define it.

So every requirement string in the system is refused, always, and each caller reports that as its
own kind of failure: errSecCSResourcesInvalid here, -50 in isAppleDeveloperCert.

WHAT THESE TRACES ADD, all under CIDER_TRACE_SECITEM.

The parser plugin now says whether the framework bundle was found, where it is loading the plugin
from, whether the load succeeded, and whether the ENTRY POINT resolved -- which is a separate failure
from the load, and the first version of this trace sat before the lookup and so said "loaded" while
the lookup was failing.

The parser's OWN error text is printed. It was being thrown into a CFError nobody reads, and that
string is what names the syntax the grammar did not like.

validateNestedCode prints the requirement it was given and the status, instead of reporting a plist
problem for a parser problem.

ResourceSeal names which of its four identical refusals fired, and the collecting context names every
resource problem as it is gathered rather than only the one status thrown at the end. Both stayed
silent here, which is how the search narrowed to the requirement parser.
